---
title: Install
description: Download the Latent AppImage, make it executable, and run it. Linux x86_64 today; macOS later.
---

Latent ships as an [AppImage](https://appimage.org) — a single executable file with no install
step and no package manager involved. Linux on x86_64 today. macOS is planned; there are no
Windows builds.

## Download and run

Grab the latest build from the
[releases page](https://github.com/neoworks-dev/latent/releases/latest), mark it executable and
start it:

```sh
chmod +x Latent-x86_64.AppImage
./Latent-x86_64.AppImage
```

That is the whole install. To remove Latent, delete the file.

## Keeping it somewhere sensible

An AppImage runs from wherever it sits, but it is easier to find if you keep it with your other
local binaries:

```sh
mkdir -p ~/.local/bin
mv Latent-x86_64.AppImage ~/.local/bin/latent
chmod +x ~/.local/bin/latent
```

With `~/.local/bin` on your `PATH`, `latent` then starts it from any shell.

## Building from source

Latent is open source. The repository is at
[github.com/neoworks-dev/latent](https://github.com/neoworks-dev/latent); build instructions live
in its README, which is the authoritative copy as the toolchain moves.

## First run

Open a folder with **Folder…** or individual files with **Files…** in the Library sidebar. Latent
reads what is already on disk — there is no import step that copies your photos into a catalogue
of its own. See [Library & culling](/docs/latent/library) for what happens next.
